{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/515280-i-need-help-liking-this-game","id":515280,"title":"I need help liking this game....","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-05-15T10:56:28-07:00","id":515280,"title":"I need help liking this game....","content":"I'm trying. Really. One of my big issues with the 1st edition was that the tribes weren't actually tribes. Not in the truest sense. And that would be ok, if I could figure out what they actually were. Are they like denominations of the same religion? Mystery cults? I have no frame of reference. Stew Wilson said the closest thing would be branches of Free Masonry. Well, I'm not a free mason. So I don't really get that either. The whole process of how a Werewolf goes about joining a tribe seems a bit... I hate to say &quot;unrealistic&quot; when referencing a game about Werewolves... but, yeah. So, I undergo my first change and (if I'm lucky) I'm discovered by another pack of Werewolves before I cause too much carnage. Ok, cool. I'm with you so far. Then they tell me that I'm expected to join a tribe and to pick one. Like, soon. When, in all likelihood, I MIGHT know one or two members of said tribe with only a modicum of information about them. How do I even know what it means to be a Blood Talon/Storm Lord/whatever? Maybe most (smart) young Werewolves spend month or years making that decision, and actually travel around looking to meet other members of their potential tribe before making a decision. But that doesn't appear to be implied by the setting. I get that, if one particular tribe dominates a certain area, new Werewolves are more likely to join that tribe than another. That makes sense to me. But if I'm discovered by a multi-tribal pack to begin with, I'm supposed to just pick one? I feel like the 2nd Edition tries to remedy this, somewhat, by tying the tribes to a &quot;chosen prey.&quot; But this just seems a bit contrived to me. And it creates the problem of &quot;I just found out that I'm a werewolf, how they hell do I know what my chosen prey is?&quot; And finally, I feel like the tribes just aren't archetypal enough. I feel like I could take almost any character concept and come up with a logical reason to make him a member of any tribe. So help me out here, guys. What am I missing? I love the thematic focus of the game (the wolf must hunt). But beyond that I'm just sort of meh.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":6039,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6039-mattlutz","user":"MattLutz","date":". 05-15-2015, 01:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6039-mattlutz","name":"MattLutz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-15T11:07:59-07:00","id":515305,"title":"","content":"Character creation generally assumes you've been active long enough to choose a tribe on your own, whether you've been taken in by a protectorate or a nomad of that tribe found you and saw potential in you.<br /> <br /> Obviously not all werewolves are members of tribes, and not all werewolves start as members of tribes. Like you said, most young werewolves spend time making that decision. If you spend a week between First Change and joining a tribe, your backstory should account for that. Since the tribes aren't exactly at war, even in territory where one tribe is more plentiful there are likely to be members of other tribes.<br /> <br /> The tribes specify a focus far beyond simply that of their chosen prey, each tribe is about a different thing, whether it's embracing civilization or honoring a territory or delving into the shadow.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-15T11:55:28-07:00","id":515326,"title":"","content":"Choosing a Tribe is like choosing a religion. You are exposed to it by those around you and then you either feel that this is for you or else you search out others and find that one that is right to you. Some instantly KNOW that the religion they have been seen is the best for them and others spend months or years or even their whole lifetime to find the right one for them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/786-thedonnie","name":"thedonnie","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-15T11:59:50-07:00","id":515329,"title":"","content":"Yeah, the character creation rules assume that a starting character has been a werewolf long enough to learn the ropes, explore the different tribes and what they stand for, what they have to offer, figured out which one is the best fit for them, joined a pack or formed one of their own, bonded with its totem, learned First Tongue, done a few Renown-worthy deeds, gained a few Gift Facets, and learned a few Rites, all in their background. That probably takes at least a few months, maybe even a year or more. If you want to play a character fresh off the First Change, you should make a human character, then give them an Auspice, a dot of its associated Renown, the first dot of the appropriate Moon Gift, and start at Harmony 9. Add the other parts of the Werewolf Template as they're earned in-game.<br /> <br /> As for what the tribes are, think of them like extended support networks for like-minded werewolves. Personally, I think the closest analogy is Fraternities or Sororities. And, of course, not every werewolf joins a Tribe. If you can't see any reason your character would want to associate themselves with one of the Tribes, that character might make an excellent Ghost Wolf.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-15T13:01:54-07:00","id":515362,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/515280-i-need-help-liking-this-game?p=515329#post515329\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for what the tribes are, think of them like extended support networks for like-minded werewolves. ...<br /> And, of course, not every werewolf joins a Tribe. If you can't see any reason your character would want to associate themselves with one of the Tribes, that character might make an excellent Ghost Wolf.</div> </div> </div> </div>While the Tribes aren't supposed to be archetypical per se, each does have its own ethos and overarching culture. Iron Masters are far more interested in interacting with and integrating with human culture than any of the others. Hunters in Darkness are far more interested in keeping their territory clean than any of the others. Bone Shadows are far more interested in the Hisil and its denizens than the others. Storm Lords are staunch individualists who value fortitude above all else and Blood Talons are battle-crazed warriors.<br /> Now, there's a lot more to any of them than just that, as shown in their write ups, but the point is that they all have unifying qualities and interests even outside the &quot;sacred prey&quot;. If you have an affinity for dealing with spirits and you want to grow in that, being a Bone Shadow is a good way to get in contact with people who have been doing that for years.<br /> Sure, a fresh Werewolf won't know any of that, but older ones will. Unless your wolf is all on its own, it will quickly be in contact with people who know what Tribe helps which kinds of people thrive. And they should also understand forcing someone into a Tribe who's ethos they don't intrinsically share will do nobody any good. As such, most Werewolves get pointed in the right direction reasonably quickly.<br /> As to why they're called &quot;Tribes&quot; - that's a fair question. Most &quot;tribes&quot; in the rest of the world are things you're born into. It is the nation of your birth, albiet typically a small nation. It's hard to say where you were born from as a Werewolf, though, as its unlikely that your parents were Werewolves. In the end, your Pack is your family. Tribes are, in a way, just really really big packs. Tribes are more about a joint culture than Pack are, so the analogy ends up holding up pretty well: families are to Packs as tribes/nations are to Tribes.<br /> <br /> That all said in the Ghost Wolves writeup in the book, they view the various Tribes as creepy wolf-cults. It's a totally reasonable stance, and if its yours, maybe you would make a good Ghost Wolf.<br /> <br /> More than anything, don't get hung up on that one word. The game has a lot to offer and the nomenclature isn't really all that important.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4563-terraben","name":"TerraBen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"278"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-15T13:05:52-07:00","id":515363,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MattLutz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/515280-i-need-help-liking-this-game#post515280\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How do I even know what it means to be a Blood Talon/Storm Lord/whatever? Maybe most (smart) young Werewolves spend month or years making that decision, and actually travel around looking to meet other members of their potential tribe before making a decision. But that doesn't appear to be implied by the setting.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's implied well enough. How do kids start to dream about becoming firefighters, jet pilots, astronauts? Stories. The oral tradition of the setting should account for these. Make up your legends, be they alive or long dead, examples of tribesmen which the characters would see fit to emulate - the Takezo Tensei to our Hiro.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MattLutz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/515280-i-need-help-liking-this-game#post515280\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But if I'm discovered by a multi-tribal pack to begin with, I'm supposed to just pick one? I feel like the 2nd Edition tries to remedy this, somewhat, by tying the tribes to a &quot;chosen prey.&quot; But this just seems a bit contrived to me. And it creates the problem of &quot;I just found out that I'm a werewolf, how they hell do I know what my chosen prey is?&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>There isn't nearly as much hand-holding in Uratha society as there is in the Western one. Your choices, your prey, your heroes, your deeds and your failures. Save for the classical Fire-Touched who would have you convert or die, Forsaken aren't like the army, where you sign your name on the dotted lines and your in, for better or worse. Being in a tribe carries it's own blessings, and responsibilities, hence why you must prove yourself worthy of one.<br /> <br /> My pack wouldn't go around trying to net converts to any tribe, save for the actually gifted.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MattLutz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/515280-i-need-help-liking-this-game#post515280\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And finally, I feel like the tribes just aren't archetypal enough. I feel like I could take almost any character concept and come up with a logical reason to make him a member of any tribe. So help me out here, guys. What am I missing? I love the thematic focus of the game (the wolf must hunt). But beyond that I'm just sort of meh.</div> </div> </div> </div>Play a Ghost Wolf who has all those same issues. Being in a tribe isn't required at all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-15T14:14:38-07:00","id":515435,"title":"","content":"I appreciate all of the replies. They were all helpful. I guess I just didn't catch the implication, in reading through 2nd edition, that you're assumed to have been a Werewolf for a while. I didn't get that vibe from 1st edition, and I guess my experience is colored by being a long time WtA player/storyteller where beginning characters are assumed to be fresh from their rite of passage in most cases. And I do think I'm getting hung up on the word &quot;tribe&quot; too much. I was having a hard time imagining why, once I've decided to become a Storm Lord/Blood Talon/whatever, I would decide to form a pack with Werewolves from other tribes. Why not stick to my own tribe, since we all think the same? But I think it helps to think of the Tribes more as religious denominations or affiliations. Its what you do for your own personal spiritual fulfillment, but that doesn't bleed over into your social life necessarily. Most Catholics (or Methodists or whatever) don't choose to ONLY be friends with other Catholics and Methodists. They meet with them from time to time (on Sundays) to discuss spiritual matters, but they have their own lives outside of church. Is that a fair comparison?<br /> <br /> And, for the record, yeah I would probably play a Ghost Wolf. You guys have me pegged. I'm the storyteller, more often than not, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":6039,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6039-mattlutz","user":"MattLutz","date":". 05-15-2015, 05:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6039-mattlutz","name":"MattLutz","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-15T14:45:19-07:00","id":515452,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MattLutz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/515280-i-need-help-liking-this-game?p=515435#post515435\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I appreciate all of the replies. They were all helpful. I guess I just didn't catch the implication, in reading through 2nd edition, that you're assumed to have been a Werewolf for a while. I didn't get that vibe from 1st edition, and I guess my experience is colored by being a long time WtA player/storyteller where beginning characters are assumed to be fresh from their rite of passage in most cases. And I do think I'm getting hung up on the word &amp;quot;tribe&amp;quot; too much. I was having a hard time imagining why, once I've decided to become a Storm Lord/Blood Talon/whatever, I would decide to form a pack with Werewolves from other tribes. Why not stick to my own tribe, since we all think the same? But I think it helps to think of the Tribes more as religious denominations or affiliations. Its what you do for your own personal spiritual fulfillment, but that doesn't bleed over into your social life necessarily. Most Catholics (or Methodists or whatever) don't choose to ONLY be friends with other Catholics and Methodists. They meet with them from time to time (on Sundays) to discuss spiritual matters, but they have their own lives outside of church. Is that a fair comparison?</div> </div> </div> </div>That's pretty fair. I don't think of it quite like denominations of a parent religion personally, but I don't think you'd be far off the mark to grok it that way. The reason I compared it to a fraternity is that a fraternity, like a tribe, is sort of an extended family that you chose, rather than being born into. Other members are your brothers and sisters, even if you share no (recent) blood connection and may hail from completely different parts of the world. You see another Hunter in Darkness, and even if you've never met them before, you know they share your respect for territory. You know they've earned their place in your family by virtue of their Purity. You know they believe, as you do, that the Sartha are the greatest threat to the delicate balance between spirit and flesh, and are therefore the prey most worthy of Siskur-Dah. Those shared beliefs form a feeling kinship and camaraderie.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And, for the record, yeah I would probably play a Ghost Wolf. You guys have me pegged. I'm the storyteller, more often than not, though. </div> </div> </div>I feel you there. I always have the most fun playing newly First Changed Uratha, who still see all these other werewolves as crazy and still want to have a normal life. Fortunately, 2e's new approach to Harmony not only allows for that Wolfman style of werewolf, it also makes the transition to eventually accepting your spiritual side more natural and versimilar, at least to me. YMMV.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-15T14:58:25-07:00","id":515464,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MattLutz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/515280-i-need-help-liking-this-game?p=515435#post515435\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was having a hard time imagining why, once I've decided to become a Storm Lord/Blood Talon/whatever, I would decide to form a pack with Werewolves from other tribes. Why not stick to my own tribe, since we all think the same?</div> </div> </div> </div>Location is a big influence. Where you live may not be where there's only members of your tribe. Why should a Storm Lord upend his life to move to your town? Or you to theirs?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But I think it helps to think of the Tribes more as religious denominations or affiliations. Its what you do for your own personal spiritual fulfillment, but that doesn't bleed over into your social life necessarily. Most Catholics (or Methodists or whatever) don't choose to ONLY be friends with other Catholics and Methodists. They meet with them from time to time (on Sundays) to discuss spiritual matters, but they have their own lives outside of church. Is that a fair comparison? </div> </div> </div>I've heard a lot of people associate it with religion, and there is that aspect especially with the totem, but I always associated it as something more similar to, say, higher learning. Some people choose to go to college to become doctors, some artists, some engineers. In the course of their lives and when hanging out with their friends they put their education to work, even when not 'on the job.' Each may have different views about what's important in things, and forgive the simple stereotypes, the doctor overall health of people, the engineer the usefulness and pared down structure of a thing, the artist the beauty of it. Similar to how a werewolf may view sacred prey. Each tribe thinks 'their' sacred prey is the most dangerous, it says this in the first line under Prey in the tribe write-ups, with the Hunters in the Darkness being a sort of combo-breaker for style.<br /> <br /> And while it's likely a group of friends who are doctors or engineers are going to be able to come up with some pretty cool stuff on their own if they associate their hobby with their skills, they might miss out on things other people could bring to the table. Just like a pack who focus solely on one type of sacred prey can miss the dangers of the others. Though, as always must be stated, you're not confined to only hunting the sacred prey of your tribe.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-15T19:32:11-07:00","id":515694,"title":"","content":"On the matter of &quot;why would a multi-tribe Pack exist&quot;, part of it is location as nofather said. Another part is pragmatism. Even though the Hunters (and Iron Masters) care most deeply about their territory, its important to everyone. And everyone realizes that while thier sacred prey is the most dangerous, it's not the only threat around. Much like the doctor/engineer/artist suggestion, it's good to have friends around that know how to deal with a variety of problems. At any given time, you can take the lead at what you're good at, but your physically-immediate in-group won't be faced with out of context problems as often if it includes people with multiple viewpoints.<br /> <br /> That said, the idea of single-Tribe packs is a highly supported one, especially in certain areas. I remember reading in one of the fictions where one Werewolf was reminiscing and said &quot;I came from a place where being a Uratha meant being a Blood Talon.&quot; If you've got a relatively small territory bounded by the territory of other Packs, then maybe you can afford to have blind spots and let other (allied) Packs clean up the messes you can't handle. In exchange, you'll be masters of whatever your Tribe focuses on and can deal with that for everyone around.<br /> <br /> The main reason there seems to be a focus on multi-Tribe packs is the Don't Say No commandment. If your group of players feel pulled to different Tribes, then let them play different Tribes, even if it doesn't make a ton of sense from some perspectives. They tried really hard to get it to make sense.<br /> Or, since you're the Storyteller, you could say &quot;hey guys, we're gonna play a single-Tribe Pack&quot; and see if they can agree on one. That could be an interesting story, too, if some people are chafing at their Tribal expectations and, as a group, they lack the tools to do certain jobs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4563,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4563-terraben","user":"TerraBen","date":". 05-15-2015, 10:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4563-terraben","name":"TerraBen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"278"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-15T21:19:40-07:00","id":515791,"title":"","content":"Here is an analogy to help you understand a Tribe. Think of a Tribe like a Motorcycle Club. The Sons of Anarchy for example. Everyone in the club is bound by the ethos of the club and treat each other like an extended family even if they run in separate packs. Their &quot;totem&quot; Samcro unites all Sons of Anarchy under a common bond and code of conduct. However, a Firstborn Totem, unlike Samcro, is alive and uses spiritual power to spiritually bind members together.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5700-nuwisha-nutjob","name":"Nuwisha Nutjob","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5700_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-16T03:16:25-07:00","id":516007,"title":"","content":"When considering the tribes, it is important to take into account the basic situation of a newly changed werewolf and of the setting at a larger scale.<br /> <br /> <br /> You've changed and you've probably killed someone. You already know what it's like to be hunted - strange shadowy beings stalking you, trying to kill you. Spirits and rats infesting your home and trying to get close to the people you care about. You've probably already fought for your life once or twice - and won, obviously, but it doesn't take much smarts to figure out that the next time could well be different.<br /> <br /> <br /> Your skin doesn't act the way it used to, and neither do your emotions, senses and appetites, even. You can't help looking at other people and, sometimes, thinking &quot;food&quot;. You feel claustrophobic in your human skin, and when previously you felt like hitting somebody who pissed you off, now it's a challenge not to tear his throat out.<br /> <br /> This is where the tribes come in. This is what the tribes have to offer:<br /> <br /> <br /> As a werewolf, you'll have to fight for your life; probably on a regular basis. The Blood Talons show you how. They know how to protect yourself and those close to you; Fenris-Ur will teach you how to be an effective warrior. They also give you ways how to deal with the burning rage affecting all werewolves. The Blood Talons are the ones you approach when you don't want to wake up in the blood of your husband one day, and when you want to survive the next time the Predator Kings show up from nowhere and start biting huge chunks of flesh out of your flesh.<br /> <br /> <br /> This scary, hostile world of predator and prey you are now a part of? The Bone Shadows best understand how it works and how to make sense of it. They explain spirits. They tell you which places in the Shadow to avoid and where you'll be safe. Death Wolf provides you with the means of fighting your enemies with ways other than using your claws. And they also show you how to make sense of the death and pain and grief that always seems to follow the Forsaken around. They provide meaning in a world that doesn't appear to have any, at the first glance.<br /> <br /> <br /> There's a wolf in your heart, your bones and your flesh, and it'll never go away. The Hunters in Darkness know that wolf better than any other tribe. They know how to make sense of the rage, the new senses, instincts, demands of the wolf-heart. They also teach you how to hunt effectively, and how to survive when you're prey. Things like where to best hide, how to best track your prey, how to kill prey in as little time as possible are natural to the children of the Black Wolf.<br /> <br /> <br /> Werewolves are not human. The human skin doesn't change their basic nature; but despite the wolf-heart and spirit-soul there's still a human mind. After the traumatic experience of the First Change, which more often than not, ends with lots of blood, the Iron Masters are the ones who help a young werewolf to reintegrate into the human culture and world and to reconcile wolf-heart with human-mind. They're the ones who know how a wolf hides and hunts among sheep. Red Wolf knows the spirits that hunt in the cities and close to humanity, and his children will teach you how to hunt with cunning, how to adapt to situations that cannot otherwise be dealt with.<br /> <br /> <br /> The Storm Lords don't pick you up when you're weak. They won't comfort you in your time of need. Winter Wolf does not care about your weakness and failings. The Storm Lords cannot soothe any pain you feel from the First Change or from being what you are. The Winter Wolf will instead be here in your moments of strength and endurance. His children show you how to be your best, strongest self - how to stare this cold, dark, hostile world that wants you dead at every turn, in the face, and how to make it back down with an alpha's growl. They know how to fight. They know the spirit world, the worlds of wolf and men. All of these things are part of their responsibility.<br /> <br /> <br /> ... still three tribes left to cover!<br /> <br /> <br /> You've been beaten and bruised by this world. There's nothing that feels right. Friends? Jobs? Relationships? Family? Nothing has ever worked out for you. The world of men has let you down and failed you more often than you can count.<br /> <br /> No more. Something's got to give.<br /> <br /> Your old life is gone. You might have killed those you love during your first change. You're a monster and a hunter now. There's no way back.<br /> It's not your fault. It's THEM. This world isn't how it should be.<br /> There is hope. You've got the oldest, nastiest, toughest of all Wolf Gods on your side, giving you strength, rage, purpose and a mission - rebuild this world in your - in his - image. Make them all pay what they did to you. It's what you should do. It's what's natural to do.<br /> <br /> <br /> The First Change isn't the end of your old life, it's you reaching your destiny and becoming a true member of your family. It's when you realize your potential and the universe gives you what you're owed.<br /> The Ivory Claws teach you how people are not born equal. There are those whose blood and ancestry matters more. You're one of the lucky ones. You're special - the purest of the Pure. You're better than other people. It's quite simply genetics.<br /> This world belongs to its true rulers. The Chosen Ones, those selected by Silver Wolf to run this world, must take command.<br /> Go hunt those who would refuse to obey.<br /> <br /> <br /> Spirits stalk and try to kill you. The Hosts are at your heels, only waiting to take you down once you're weak. Werewolves hunt you as the competition you are.<br /> It's good, so very good, to have friends in this world. People who look out for you. Spirits who let you live as long as you abase yourself before them instead of attempting, vainly, to keep them in check. The Fire-Touched have more to offer than just protection from the spirits, strength in numbers and sympathetic ears for all your troubles and worries. They also have faith, insight and the certainty that their cause is the right one. And they're always, without exception, honest to you - Rabid Wolf sees lying as one of the worst of sins.<br /> Only a lunatic would refuse to take their offer and join them. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/286-christian-a","name":"Christian A","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"832"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-16T07:59:35-07:00","id":516192,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MattLutz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/515280-i-need-help-liking-this-game?p=515435#post515435\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I appreciate all of the replies. They were all helpful. I guess I just didn't catch the implication, in reading through 2nd edition, that you're assumed to have been a Werewolf for a while.</div> </div> </div> </div>Note that you have been a werewolf long enough to:<br /> <br /> 1) Lower your Harmony from 9 (where it starts after First Change) to 7.<br /> 2) Commit deeds worthy of Renown three times.<br /> 3) Get two or three Gifts out of spirits (probably by partaking in a Siskur-Dah).<br /> <br /> When I ran Werewolf in first edition, I had the characters tell me what they got their three starting Renown for, in order to establish what they had done so far as werewolves. As the book notes, doing something too boss for one of your starting points can really screw you later, since you are expected to one-up yourself. With the new edition, I'm also going to have them describe how they acquired their Gifts and possibly what they had to do to bring themselves closer to Harmony (look at that list of spirit Breaking Points and tell me there aren't great background stories to be had in stuff like spending a week in <i>Hisil</i> or killing a human).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Eric Christian Berg</b><br /> <i>Onyx Path Freelancer</i><br /> Promethean: The Created Second Edition, Dark Eras Companion","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2019-eberg","name":"eberg","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2019_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"284"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-17T14:12:36-07:00","id":517341,"title":"","content":"I think we all get a bit hung up on what we expect tribe to mean vs what it actually means.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> WoD-Dark Eras!! (Backed for Viking Age Werewolf)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1939-lyrics-of-war","name":"Lyrics Of War","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1939_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1769"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-23T13:57:41-07:00","id":525877,"title":"","content":"If your problem is actually the question of why your character would join this tribe, as opposed to the player who has read the book and judged the advantages, then flip the coin. Each tribe follows one of the first born and he picks you. Storm Wolf appears to you in the rain, Death Wolf haunts your dreams, one way or another they call you to where you belong. A Ghost Wolf either doesn't receive a calling or chooses to reject the one he does.<br /> As for thinking of tribes as a religion, the Forsaken are the religion the tribes are just parts of a greater whole. To use the Catholic analogy the tribes are like Jesuit priests vs Benedictine monks, all part of the same church yet fulfilling different roles within it. That's why multi tribe packs work, they all serve the same cause, they just have different parts to play.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-24T03:42:12-07:00","id":526519,"title":"","content":"As to 2ED changes - <b>Sacred Prey of the Tribes can be most important point on choosing the group</b>. You come from First Change and we know it's very bloody mess there. Does other werewolves stalked you then and still hunting you to this day? Blood Talons will help you get them of you, once and for all. Spirits possessed your loved one, making you to kill him in your first Death Rage? Bone Shadows will teach you all you need about spirits and how to fight them, so next innocents would not becomes they victims. Your family home has been infested by two meters rats and they tried to put you as sacrifice to their King? Now you can learn art of deratization with Hunters in Darkness - and return your families home to previous glory. Humans know you are werewolf and hunts you after the massacre you did in the Gauru form you first assumed? Iron Masters will teach you to become invisible in mortals society, to hunt among the sheeps and even take out those that lead humans to they own destruction. Those blasphemous Clamied destroyed your personal life that was so ideal to this day? You will retake it with help of Storm Lords and their righteousness kills.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-24T12:23:48-07:00","id":526933,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/515280-i-need-help-liking-this-game?p=526519#post526519\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As to 2ED changes - <b>Sacred Prey of the Tribes can be most important point on choosing the group</b>.</div> </div> </div> </div>It <i>can</i> be, but it definitely doesn't <i>have</i> to be. I agree that your examples are all great reasons for a character to join a tribe (although I would argue that loved ones being possessed is just as much a reason to join Storm Lords as the Bone Shadows). But just as many new werewolves may join for other reasons, and the attitudes toward their Sacred Prey may be learned afterwards. For example, one character I've been working on joined the Hunters in Darkness because he didn't have a home prior to his First Change. After becoming a werewolf, his territory was extremely important to him, because it wasn't <i>just</i> where he hunted, it was the only home he had. Now that he had a place he could finally call his own (even if it was just some patch of woods he had cleared out and formed a locus on), he wanted to protect it at any and all costs. It was sacred to him, and he identified strongly with the vow to let no sacred place in your territory be violated. He would have felt that way no matter what, so the Hunters in Darkness were a perfectly natural fit for him. He joined for the support of other werewolves who shared his reverence for sacred territory, and could help him learn to better protect his. It was only after joining that he learned of the horrible swarms of vermin that could infest a territory and wreak havoc on its spiritual equilibrium by tearing apart or sealing shut the gauntlet. And of course, feeling as he does about the importance of territory, of course he would see such creatures as the prey most deserving of <i>Siskur-Dah</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-24T18:44:52-07:00","id":527230,"title":"","content":"Could have gone Iron Master as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-24T22:45:53-07:00","id":527361,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ephsy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/515280-i-need-help-liking-this-game?p=527230#post527230\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Could have gone Iron Master as well.</div> </div> </div> </div>Definitely could have, yes. The deciding factor for me in reality was getting Warding as an Affinity Gift. But in-character it's because the emphasis on protecting the territory as opposed to honoring it. He definitely could have gone either way, but it still serves as an example of how the favored prey can follow the choice of Tribe just as easily as the choice of Tribe can follow the favored prey.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}